.TH AMRTEST 1
.SH NAME
amrtest \- test block-structured adaptive mesh refinement
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B amrtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Amrtest
checks
.IR amr (2),
which had no test at all.
.PP
What it checks was chosen from what that module claims rather than from
what is convenient to drive.
Refining a block and counting the result is easy and exercises almost
none of it; what is hard in block AMR is everything that spans levels.
.SS What is checked
The
.B 2:1 balance
that the whole design rests on: no two face-adjacent leaves may differ by
more than one level, which is what bounds
.B exchange
to a same-level, one-coarser, or up-to-four-finer neighbour per face.
This is a scan over every leaf and all six of its faces, walking the tree
to find whatever covers the neighbouring region, and it runs after
.I every
adaptation pass rather than only at the end \(em a pass that breaks
balance and a later one that happens to restore it is not the same as
never breaking it.
.PP
That
.B refine
followed by
.B coarsen
returns exactly what was there.
Injection then volume-average is the identity on any field, so this uses
values that differ in every cell; a uniform field would pass against a
.B coarsen
that simply copied one child.
.PP
That a
.B constant survives
both a cross-level exchange and a diffusion step.
Prolongation, restriction and the Laplacian are all exact on a constant
whatever the level structure, so any cross-level mistake appears as a
named cell that is no longer that constant, rather than as a plausible
field that happens to be wrong.
.SS Conservation, measured three ways
Mass is treated as two different questions, because lumping them
together would make the strict one useless.
.PP
With no coarse-fine interface anywhere \(em a uniform forest at level 0,
and one with every block refined \(em diffusion under the zero-Neumann
boundary that
.B exchange
applies is conservative, and the mass must not move.
It does not: about
.BR 1e\-14 .
That is a real invariant and a stencil or boundary mistake breaks it.
.PP
Across interfaces
.IR amr (2)
documents its prolongation as not flux-conservative, so mass does move.
It is measured rather than asserted tightly: about
.B 5%
over twenty sweeps on a 608-leaf forest adapted around a ball.
The bound in the test is loose, and exists to catch a change in
behaviour rather than because any particular figure is right.
A drift of
.I zero
there is also a failure, since it would mean either that the forest has
no interface or that
.B totalmass
is not measuring mass.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/amrtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR amr (2),
.IR pde (2),
.IR pdetest (1)
.SH BUGS
Nothing checks
.BR coarsen 's
interaction with balance.
.IR Amr (2)
states plainly that coarsening never checks cross-neighbour balance, so a
forest can in principle be left unbalanced by coarsening alone; this
test's balance scan would catch it, but nothing here drives
.B adapt
with a criterion that shrinks, which is what would provoke it.
.PP
One block size, one domain, one criterion shape, diffusion only.
.PP
The interface drift figure is for the specific forest built here.
It is not a property of the module, and a different refinement pattern
will give a different number.
